\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sidereal TimeBefore Railroads began upsetting the habits of people, each town or community had its own\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain time, and there was no serious effort toward standardization. TRUE LOCAL TIME was\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain employed. If the Sun crossed the meridian, it was noon. When it was noon in New York City, it\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain was 11:56 A.M. in Philadelphia, ll:4 A.M. in Washington D.C., 12:04 P.M. in Hartford,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Connecticut, and l2:l2 P.M. in Boston, Mass. This would seem quite confusing today, but it did\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain not at the time, because few peop1e traveled from one of these towns to another. If you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain were born in Boston and you were like most folks, you lived in Boston and you died in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Boston, and you had little interest in what the time might be in Hartford. Only Indians lived in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain California, and they didn't care.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain As railroads began shortening the distance between towns, there had to be some\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain standardization or a conductor would be changing his watch as he left each station. As a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain result, the earth was divided into 24 different time zones, corresponding with the 24 hours\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain into which we divide each day, because folks couldn't get away from the fact that when it was\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain midnight in one place, it was noon in another. When we state the time of day, we are merely\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain giving an expression as to the relationship of one earth location to the Sun. This becomes\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain very important in astrology, and when we express the time of a birth or an event it is quite\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain essential that we know what kind of time we are ta1king about. It might be True Local Time,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Greenwich Time, Standard Time, Daylight Saving Time or War Time. If we do not get this\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain straight, a chart is incorrectly erected and we are looking at something that is not true.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It is customary to divide a circle into 360 equal parts and each of these parts is called a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain degree, and so we say there are 360 degrees to a circle, regardless of the size of the circle. It\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain may be a circle an inch in diameter or a million miles in diameter. It still contains 360 degrees.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain There is no particular reason for dividing a circle into 360 degrees. It is generally believed\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain that this figure came about because people believed there were 360 days in a year, which\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain would make the average daily apparent motion of the Sun in the heavens one degree per\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain day, but this is guess work. It may or may not be true.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain All such measurements are actually arbitrary, but we adopt them so that each of us knows\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain what the other is talking about. As to how accurate a measurement should be, it all depends\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain upon the use to which it is to be put. Although in mechanics, it is often necessary to measure\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain something to an accuracy of 1/10,000th of an inch, you would be little concerned about an\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain error of an inch or even a foot if you were measuring the distance from New York to San\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Francisco.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain The circle is further subdivided into smaller parts by dividing a degree into 60 minutes of arc\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain and a minute into 60 seconds of arc. Minutes and seconds of arc should not be confused with\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain minutes and seconds of time. In one case you are measuring time and in the other case you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain are measuring ratio of a complete circle. While there are 86,40O seconds of time to a day,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain there are 1,296,000 seconds of arc to a circle. Since there are 360 Degrees to a complete\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain circle and 60 minutes to each degree, there are 360 x 60 or 21,600 minutes to a complete\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain circle.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Since there are 60 seconds to a minute of arc, there are 60 x 60 seconds (3600) to a degree\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of arc, or 1,296,000 seconds of arc to a circle. You do not have to memorize these figures.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain They are only intended to impress you with the fact that there are a lot of seconds of arc to a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain circle, and that a second of arc is a very tiny measurement, which in most cases must not be\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain regarded too seriously. For some purposes in astronomy it would have to be regarded very\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain seriously.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain In non-mystical astrology, it is seldom necessary to be more accurate than to the degree.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain There are mystical methods of astrology, which are difficult to prove or disprove, where\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain accuracy to minutes of arc are essential, but these systems are largely hypothetical with no\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain statistical evidence at this time to back them up. From a practical point-of-view, the student\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain will do better to ignore such systems until he knows what he can do with systems that do not\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain demand such hair-splitting accuracy. In other words, if you are going from New York to San\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Francisco, let us not bother trying to measure the distance in units of ten thousandths of an\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain inch. It's like trying to count the grains of sand on the beach. You won't live that long. We are\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain often faced with the paradox of an astrologer computing a birth horoscope to the exact\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain degree, minute and second of arc, from birth time that is not accurately known to within a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain half hour. It is important to learn how much you can do with astrology without bothering with\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain these split second calculations, for you will often find that the split- second calculator, while\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain very impressive, turns up in the end with the wrong answer. Its like an evening that the writer\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain spent at a local meeting of the Arnerican Statistical Association. A German mathematician\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain who did not speak clear English devoted hours explaining how a problem could be done in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain two different ways. He illustrated by doing the problem on a blackboard both ways, and at\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the end of the evening obtained two different answers. It was too late to do the problem\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain again, and the German mathematician was returning to Germany. We never did find out the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain right answer.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It is important to simplify truth, which is seldom accomplished, and while this will be our\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain purpose, we will apologize in advance if our own efforts are not all that is to be desired. The\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain important thing to realize is that for all practical purposes, a person can go a long way with\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain astrology without bothering with split-second calculations, and in many cases, the astrologers\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain who are most insistent on such calculations are consistently wrong in their interpretations\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain because they never have learned nor discovered the true basic principles of astrology.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Meanwhile, we have often found that some of our best astrologers were women who had\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain only the most fundamental conception of mathematics. It isn't unusual for us to find an\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain uneducated housewife who grasps the fundamentals of astrology far better than an\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain individual with an almost endless string of college degrees. She has not learned so many\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain things things are not so.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain One important thing for you to remember is that there are 360 degrees to a circle, 60\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain minutes to a degree and 60 seconds to a minute of arc, and when you are measuring\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain something to a second of arc, you are dividing the circle into l,296,000 parts.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We measure the circle of the heavens in degrees and we measure the surface of the earth in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain degrees. East and West measurements are called LONGITUDE, while north and south\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain measurements are called LATITUDE when we are measuring the surface of the earth\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain LONGITUDE is measured east or west from a line running north and south from\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain GREENWICH, England. Thus, we say that New York is 74 degrees West Longitude, Chicago is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 88 degrees West Longitude, and Los Angeles is ll8 degrees West Longitude, while Berlin is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 13 degrees East Longitude, Moscow 36 degrees East Longitude, and Tokyo 140 degrees East\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Longitude. When you reach 180 degrees West Longitude, you meet l80 degrees EAST\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Longitude and you are at the International Date Line, where it is one day on one side of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain line and another day on the other side.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Longitudinal lines run from the north to the south poles. Two such lines a degree apart would\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain be about 70 miles apart at the equator, 35 miles apart at 45 degrees north or south of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain equator, and zero degrees apart where they meet at the poles. All such lines meet at the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain po1es.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain The heavens are also measured off in degrees, minutes and seconds of Longitude, starting at\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain that point we call 0-Aries and continuing around the zodiac in a counterclockwise direction.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain For navigation and other purposes, they are measured from the same point but around the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain celestial equator, which means an extension of our earthly equator into space. This type of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain measurement, which will not concern you very much, is called RIGHT ASCENSION. It is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain abbreviated R.A..\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain LATITUDE is measured in degrees north or south of the equator. Miami is 26 degrees North\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Latitude, Atlanta or Los Angeles, 34 degrees North Latitude, San Francisco 38 and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Washington D. C. 39 degrees North Latitude, Malone, N. Y. 45 degrees North Latitude, and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain therefore half way to the north pole. The poles of the earth are 90 degrees from the equator.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Capetown, S. Africa is 34 degrees SOUTH Latitude, Buenos Aires 35 degrees South Latitude,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Stanley in the Falkland Islands 52 degrees South Latitude.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Oddly, although we measure earthly Latitude from the equator, we measure celestial Latitude\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain from the ecliptic, zodiac, or the path of the earth's orbit. When we measure north or south of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain a projection of the equator in the heavens, we do not call it Latitude but Declination. In other\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain words, 45 degrees North Declination in the sky would be exactly over 45 degrees North\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Latitude on the earth. Celestial Declination is the equivalent of earthly Latitude, while\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain celestial Latitude is something else again. This is an in-consistency of the language of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain astronomy.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain The north pole is 90 degrees North Latitude for ANY Longitude, since all Longitudinal lines\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain meet at the north pole. These Longitudinal lines run from the north to the south pole, while\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain LATITUDINAL lines are parallel circles running all around the earth. The equator itself is the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 0-Latitude line. A little circle one degree in radius with the north pole as its center will mark\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain off 9 degree. North Latitude. A little larger circle two degrees in radius with the north pole as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain center will be degrees Worth Latitude. We have mentioned the north pole as the center of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain these circles inaccurately to better make the matter clear to you. Actually, the center of all\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Latitudinal circles must be considered the axis of the earth itself.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain This information is necessary before we can begin to erect the chart, which, as far back as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain history will take us, has been called the horoscope. The word actually means HOUR SCOPE\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain or H0UR CHART, and therefore would mean a chart accurate to within an hour. However, in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain common usage, it means anything from a chart erected for the exact minute to a newspaper\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain column about astrology, and some astrologers can waste an entire evening fighting for their\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain own personal conceptions of the meaning of the word.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We are well off, when we can know the zodiacal degree that was rising at the moment of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain birth at the particular location of birth, and that means knowing the time within a time-area\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of four minutes. The average time it takes a degree of the zodiac to cross the horizon is four\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain minutes. You can figure that out for yourself. 360 degrees at minutes to a degree is 1440\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain minutes, and 24 hours of 60 minutes each equal 1440 minutes also.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We must allow for the fact that birth times have seldom been recorded accurately, and until\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain recent years they were a matter of memory. When the child is born, the doctor is busy, and he\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain or the nurse often record the time when the excitement is over, and they may estimate how\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain long the excitement lasted. In one such case, the father who recorded the time with a stop-\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain watch when he heard the first cry from outside the delivery room, recorded the time 10\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain minutes earlier than the physician who had been asked for an accurate time by the father\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain who was an astrologer.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Down through the ages, the moment of birth has been considered by astrologers to be when\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the child first breaths as a separate entity, on his own, when he is, biologically, no longer a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain part of, nor dependent upon the mother for his oxygen. His life is then underway. He is on his\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain own, and he has to keep those lungs pumping air. Scientists approaching astrology for the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain first time usually want to know why a horoscope is not erected for the moment of conception\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain instead of the moment of birth. Who knows the moment of conception? The fact is that the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain functioning of the birth chart itself gives us reason to respect it. Whether or not a conception\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain chart might also have value is completely beyond our investigative ability. We can offer no\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain opinion on the matter. Bailey and Sepharial attempted to show a lunar interrelationship\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain between moment of conception and moment of birth, but there was a good deal in the way\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of errors in their work and there is too little evidence at this time to determine whether they\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain may have been right or wrong. They were both able men, and their work should ultimately be\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain carefully tested to see whether any or all of their theories may have been true or false. That\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain work has not been accomplished up to the present time.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We present a diagram that will help explain STANDARD TIME. It shows the earth divided into\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 24 equal parts or TIME ZONES. The sun is due left from the earth, immediately above the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Greenwich meridian. Therefore, it is Noon at Greenwich. You will note that 15 degrees west\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain (clockwise around the circle), it is 11:00 A.M. At another 15 degrees, or a total of 30 degrees\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain west of Greenwich9 it is 10:00 A.M. When you reach 60 degrees we (near Halifax), it is 8:00\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain A.M. At 75 degrees west, you have reached Philadelphia1 and it is 7:CO A.M. This is the zone\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain known as EASTERN STANDARD TIME.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain At 90 degrees West Longitude, you are at St. Louis. It is 6:00 A.M. CENTRAL STANDARD\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain TIME, and the Sun is rising there. At 105 degrees west Longitude, you are at Denver,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Colorado. It is 5:00 A.M. MOUNTAIN STANDARD TIME. At 120 degrees west Longitude, we\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain find that it is 4:00 A.M. PACIFIC STANDARD TIME in Fresno, California. At 150 degrees west\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Longitude, it is 2:00 A.M. in Fairbanks, Alaska.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Going the other way around the circle, it is 1:00 P.M. in Berlin 2:OO AM at Alexandria in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Egypt, 6:00 P.M. at Calcutta, India, and 9:00 P.M. in Tokyo, Japan. All of this has to do with\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain when it is Noon in Greenwich, England. If we twist our dial (the earth) 15 degrees to the left\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain or counterclockwise, which is the motion of the earth in one hour it will be one hour later in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain all of these places, and the 15-degree west Longitude line will point directly at the Sun. It will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain now be 1:00 A.M. in Greenwich, 8:00 A.M. in Philadelphia, 7:00 A.M. in St. Louis; 6:00 A.M. in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Denver, where the Sun is now rising, and 5:00 A.M. in Fresno, California. It will be midnight at\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 165 degrees east.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain These 15-degree Longitudinal TIME Lines are not the beginning but the center of a Time\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Zone. Philadelphia is the center of the EASTERN STANDARD TIME Zone. New York, Boston,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain or Portland, Maine, are in the same zone. They are off to one side of center.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Logically, a Time Zone extends 71/2 degrees east and west from center, but different\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain communities take liberties with any such rule. The whole state of Ohio is on Eastern Standard\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Time. Hazard, Ky., at 83 degrees West Longitude is on CENTRAL STANDARD TIME. The time\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain setup around the world has a great many of these inconsistencies, and they complicate the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain work of the astrologer. When someone says he was born at 6:00 A.M., the astrologer must\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain know, 6:00 A.M., WHAT KIND OF TIME? If the answer is TRUE LOCAL TIME, everything is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain simplified.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain TRUE LOCAL TIME is expressed in terms of how long it has been, or will be, before or since\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the Sun crossed overhead at that location. DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME, of course is summer\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain time, used in some places. Some psychologist apparently discovered that a man who won't\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain rise before 7:00 A.M. will rise at 6:00 A.M. if you tell him it is 7:00 A.M. Not only that, he will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain go to bed earlier at night when you change the clock.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain WAR TIME is similar to DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME, except that it was employed throughout\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the year.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain In the majority of cases, you will know what kind of time you are dealing with, and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain complications will not come up, but the Rand McNally Road Atlas, which you may have, will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain show the time lines as practiced in all parts of the country.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It is quite simple to adjust STANDARD TIME to TRUE LOCAL TIME. Find the Latitude and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Longitude of the birth location. If it happens to be a multiple of 15 degrees, and therefore on\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain a Time Zone center, no adjustment is necessary.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain If it is east or west of the Time Zone center, then an adjustment IS necessary. THE\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain ADJUSTMENT WILL BE FOUR MINUTES FOR EACH DEGREE OF LONGITUDE. If the birth\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain location is EAST of the Time Zone center, then ADD four minutes for each degree. If it is west\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of center, DEDUCT four minutes for each degree. Look at the diagram again. Note that when\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain you go east, it gets later, and when you go west it gets earlier. When commercial airplanes\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain can reach a speed of 1050 miles per hour, it will be possible to travel around the equator\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain westward with the speed at which the earth rotates. Then, it can be perpetually noon for the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain people on the plane so long as it continues at a constant rate of speed. On the other hand, if\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain another plane travels eastward at the same speed the Sun will rise every twelve hours instead\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of every twenty-four hours.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It is important to get this time business fixed in your mind. It is important to realize that for\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain every degree you go west the true Local Time is four minutes earlier, and for every degree\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain you go east, it is four minutes later. Thus, if you were traveling west at the equator, every\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain time you traveled 70 miles, you would have to set your watch back four minutes to have it\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain correct for that location. If you were at the Latitude of Malone, N.Y., you would have to do\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain this every 35 miles. If you were 89 degrees North Longitude, you would have to do it at the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain end of every 1 and 1/5 mile. At the poles, there is actually no time of day, only time of year.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain New York City is 74 degrees W.L. (West Longitude). The Time Zone center is 75 degrees W.L.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Therefore, New York is one degree east of the Time Zone center, and True Local Time in New\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain York is four minutes later than Eastern Standard Time. Washington, D.C. is 77 degrees W.L.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Therefore, it is two degrees west of the Time Zone center, and True Local Time in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Washington is eight minutes earlier than Eastern standard Time.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain If you will study the diagram you should be able to grasp this, and it you do not grasp it, ask\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain questions and allow us to explain it further. This is one thing you should make sure that you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain grasp accurately. Remember that west means earlier, while east means later. Fix this in your\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain mind by selecting some town a certain number of degrees east of you and remembering that\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain it is always later there. Then select a town to the west of you, and remember that it is always\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain earlier there. Select towns a degree or more away, and figure out just how much earlier or\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain later it is.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain You will find a list of cities over the United States, as well a list of principal cities around the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain world, in the front of the book, and it will give the Longitude and Latitude of each place.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain When you deal with a town too small to be mentioned, select the nearest town of size. In\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain most cases, there will be some town near by that is listed. If you happen to be using some\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Table of Houses that does not supply this information, it can be obtained from the WORLD\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain ALMANAC, or from THE 200 YEAR EPHEMERlS by Hugh MacCraig.\par
\pard\f0\fs22\b SIDEREAL TIME\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Now, we come to an entirely different kind of time, known as SIDEREAL TIME. This is the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain kind of time we really have to employ in astrology. We have to apply it ultimately. Just as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain NOON is when the Sun is overhead in TRUE LOCAL TIME, Sidereal Time deals with a certain\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain location on the earth in relation to the zodiac. Sidereal Time is an entirely different kind of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain time from any other you know. To compare ordinary time with Sidereal Time is not like\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain comparing Daylight Time with Standard Time. A minute of Daylight Time has the same\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain duration as a minute of Standard Time. Not so with Sidereal Time. The days are shorter, the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain hours are shorter, the minutes are shorter and the seconds are shorter. A second of Sidereal\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Time is 97+ percent as long as a second of clock time. This may not seem like much of a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain difference, but it grows very large. It causes a star to rise four minutes later every night.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain The dictionary or even textbooks will tell you that SIDEREAL TIME means STAR TIME, but\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain this is an inaccuracy, because in 13,000 years, this theme would be just 100% wrong. It will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain not make any real difference in a lifetime, but if perpetuated for 13,000 years, the 100%\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain inaccuracy would actually occur. SIDEREAL TIME is still based on an earth motion and does\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain not actually coincide with the stars. It is still a matter that is determined by the earth- Sun\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain relationship.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain For example, 0:00 Sidereal Time is when 0 degrees of Aries of the zodiac of the equinoxes is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain overhead. In the Sidereal Zodiac, based on the fixed stars, today, 0:00 Sidereal Time is when\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 6-Pisces is overhead. There is a slippage of 24 degrees since the two zodiacs coincided.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Dogma has played an important part in molding certain parts of civilization. Modern\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain astronomy, which long endeavored to pass off astrology as superstition, because this was\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain originally demanded by the dogma of the church (which astronomy has forgotten) continues\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain to make all of its measurements from the astrological points. Instead of measuring SIDEREAL\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Time from the fixed stars, it is measured from the astrological zodiac. Although insisting on a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain star zodiac, measurements of time are all made from the old astrological zodiac of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain ancients. When the Nautical Almanac is published each year and universally employed, Right\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Ascension positions are published, but they are measured from 0-Aries of the astrological\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain zodiac. The stars are not employed as measuring-points.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Although we mention these factors, they are of no importance or significance from a practical\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain view point. The important thing is that after you determine TRUE LOCAL TIME, it is essential\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain that you convert it into SIDEREAL TIME. The HOROSCOPE must be erected from SIDEREAL\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain TIME. The above indiscrepancies are of no significance, unless you intend hanging around for\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain more than 100 years, when an error of significance would occur. In 6,500 years, an error of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 50% would occur, but in 100 years, the error would be little more than one percent. If you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain intend practicing astrology for more than 100 years, it might be well to look into the matter.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain After you have determined when you were born according to TRUE LOCAL TIME, then you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain must convert TRUE LOCAL TIME into SIDEREAL TIME. This is not complicated, so don't let it\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain scare you. We have mentioned 80 many different kinds of time that you may be confused, but\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain in that event, read this Lesson several times, and if it is not clear to you then, tell us and let us\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain go into it with you thoroughly and personally. The purpose of SIDEREAL TIME is to allow you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain to record the exact position of the earth within the zodiac at any given moment. One at the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain principal things we want to know is: WHAT PART OF THE ZODIAC WAS CROSSING THE\par
\pard\f0\fs22\b HORIZON?\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Your principal tools in astrology are your TABLE OF HOUSES and your EPHEMERIS. Your\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain TABLE OF HOUSES enables you to fix the earth within the zodiac for any given moment.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Your EPHEMERIS is a planetary TIME TABLE. Just as a railroad timetable will show you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain where your train should be at any given time, an EPHEMERIS will tell you where the planets\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain will be on any given day, or where they were on any particular day in the past. Because the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain planets move in accord with mathematical laws, it is possible to compute their positions for\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain any day, thousands of years in the past or thousands of years in the future. In a planetarium,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain where these mathematical laws are allowed to operate in a mechanism, the planetary\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain positions for any time, past or future, are supplied automatically. Astronomers often set the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain machinery running backward until they can look at the heavens at the supposed time of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain birth of Christ. They look at a conjunction of Mars, Jupiter and Saturn, and speculate upon\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain that being the star of Bethlehem.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Open your EPHEMERIS to any page. There are two pages of data for each month of each\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain year. On any left hand page, the first column gives the day of the month (TAG), and the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain second column is headed SIDEREAL TIME or STERNZEIT. Look down this column. You will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain note that the Sidereal Time changes about for minutes every day. This is actually the time it\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain takes the earth to move approximately one degree in its ellipse around the Sun, or how long\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain it takes the Sun, by apparent motion, to move approximately one degree from our earthly\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain point of view. If you look at the third column on the page, you will see that the Sun moves\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain about one degree per day. In other words, the daily change you see in one column is the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain same change you see in the other column, one expressed in time, the other in zodiacal\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain motion.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain At the top of the Sidereal Time column, you will see the letters R. M. S. These letters stand\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain for HOURS, MINUTES and SECONDS, so the Sidereal Time is expressed very accurately in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain hours, minutes and seconds. However, this is the Sidereal Time in Greenwich. The Sidereal\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Time at a given time on any particular day of the year is approximately the same for any year.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain You will note this if you examine your Ephemeris. For example, on July 1st, 1956, it is given as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 18 hours, 36 minutes and 5 seconds, while on July 1st, 1957, it is given as 18 hours, 35\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain minutes and 8 seconds, a difference of 57 seconds. This small discrepancy is due to the fact\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain that a year is not an exact number of days but is 365 and a fraction days.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Thus, if you happened to use the Sidereal Time for the right day of the year but the wrong\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain year, the error would not be sufficient to alter a chart or horoscope by one degree, because it\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain takes about four minutes on the average for one degree of the zodiac to rise. The average\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain error in the recording of a birth time is greater than this. If we were to believe in the accuracy\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of recorded birth times, we would have to conclude that 90% of the people are born exactly\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain as the minute hand of a clock touches the hour or half hour.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Remember that the Sidereal Time given is for Greenwich, England, and now, here is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain something that is very important. Some Ephemerides give the Sidereal Time at NOON while\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain others give the Sidereal Time for MIDNIGHT. You must know which you are using. In the case\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of DIE DEIJTSCHE EPHEMERIDE which we supply, SIDEREAL TIME IS GIVEN FOR NOON\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain FOR THE YEARS l900-1930 AND FOR MIDNIGHT FROM 1931 TO 1960 INCLUSIVE. This has\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain been the source of a great many errors on the part of students. If you look, for example, at\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the Sidereal Time given for July 1st, l9O8, instead of finding it as given above you will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain discover it to be given as 6 hours, 36 minutes and 35 seconds, a difference of twelve hours.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Record this fact indelibly in your mind that the Sidereal Time is given for Noon prior to 1931\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain and Midnight at the BEGINNING of the day from January 1st, 1931 on.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Just as Standard Time or True Local Time differs for a given moment around the world,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sidereal Time does the same thing. You will note that Sidereal Time runs from zero to 24\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain hours, like navigation time. It does not go up to 12 and start over again. There is no A.M. and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain P.M. When it is Noon at Greenwich, it is 6:OO A.M. in St. Louis, and when the Sidereal Time is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 12:00 at Greenwich, it will be 6:00 in St. Louis. This will work exactly the same. The difference\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain between True Local Time and Sidereal Time lies in the fact that with True Local Time, it is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain always 12:00 Noon when the Sun is directly on the meridian overhead, but with Sidereal\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Time, if it is 12:00 at Noon today, it will be 12:04 at Noon tomorrow. In other words, instead\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of the Sun being directly overhead, the same degree of the zodiac will be directly overhead.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain If you could see the zodiacal degrees, you would see the same degree overhead every time\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the Sidereal Time reached 12:00.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain If you record the Sidereal Time tonight when a fixed star is directly overhead at that same\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sidereal Time tomorrow night, that same fixed star will be directly overhead. This will work\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain during your lifetime, but there wouId ultimately be an error due to the precession of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain equinoxes. Although this will work for a fixed star, it will not work for a planet, because the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain planets move in relation to the fixed stars. The zodiac also moves in relation the fixed stars,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain but the circle is completed only once in approximately 26,000 years.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain True Local Time, Standard Time, Daylight Saving Time or War Time are all based on the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain position of the Sun in the sky, but Sidereal Time is based on the position of the zodiac in the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain sky.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Astrologers often have some very complicated methods of computing Sidereal Time which\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain are unnecessary if you clearly understand the difference between True Local Time and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sidereal Time, it you correctly visualize the difference, and if you do not when you complete\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain this Lesson, you better start asking questions.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Let us compare the Sidereal Time at Noon in Greenwich with the Sidereal Time at Noon in St,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Louis which is 90 degrees away. Noon in St. Louis is 6 hours later, one quarter of a day.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Therefore, since Sidereal Time at Noon changes approximately 4 minutes in a full 24 hour\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain day, it will change one minute during the time it takes the earth to make one quarter turn on\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain its axis, from the time that Greenwich faces the Sun until St. Louis faces the Sun. If you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain happen to ignore this factor, your error will be only one minute. Do you think you know your\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain birth time so accurately that this minute will make a difference? In any event, the error will be\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain but one-quarter of a degree in the placement of the earth within the zodiac. When you want\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain this degree of accuracy you can pay attention to this factor. Until you need that kind of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain accuracy you can forget it if you choose. If you accidentally forget it, the damage is not likely\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain to be noticed. We explain this factor, carefully, because the attempt to gain unnecessary\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain accuracy has often confused a student to the point where he never got beyond it. It you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain understand what this correction is all about, you can make it in your head. Until you are well\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain under way, you can ignore it.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It stands to reason that if the Sidereal Time is 12:00 at Noon, it is 13:00 at 1:00 P.M. If the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sidereal Time is 6:44 at Noon, it is 7:44 at 1:00 P.M. If the Sidereal Time is 6:44 at Noon, it\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain was 5:44 at ll:O0 A.M. This is simple addition and subtraction, and it is not something that\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain should trouble you very much.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain When you are ready to erect a Horoscope, simply take the Sidereal Time out of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain ephemeris, making sure whether it is the Side-real Time at Noon or Midnight. Then, it is up to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain you to compute the Sidereal Time AT BIRTH. You merely add or subtract. How long was the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain birth before or after Noon or Midnight? Make the adjustment to establish the SIDEREAL\par
\pard\f0\fs22\b TIME AT BIRTH.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Let us have an example.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain A person was born at 11:29 P.M. on June 3rd, 1905 at Cheyenne, Wyoming. The Table in the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain front of the TABLE OF HOUSES shows us that Cheyenne is at 105 degrees West Longitude,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain exactly on the center of the MOUNTAIN STANDARD TIME ZONE. Therefore, no correction is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain necessary for TRUE Local TIME, which will be exactly the same as Standard Time. The\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Ephemeris tells us that the Sidereal Time at Greenwich on June 3rd, 1905 at Noon was 4\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain hours 45 minutes and 5 seconds. Let us forget the 5 seconds and proceed.\par
\pard\f0\fs22\b SIDEREAL TIME AT NOON ..4:45\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f0\fs22\b TIME OF BIRTH .................+11:29\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f0\fs22\b SIDEREAL TIME AT BIRTH.15:74\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Because there are only 60 minutes in an hour, we can express 15:74 as 16:14. Thus, the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sidereal Time at birth was 16:14.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain This is still inaccurate to one and a traction minutes because we did not make the correction\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain in Sidereal Time for the time it took the face of the earth to turn from Greenwich to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Cheyenne, but it is highly improbably that the furnished birth time is accurate to a minute and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain a quarter. Did the physician check his watch and its accuracy prior to the birth? Was it a stop\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain watch? Did somebody press the button at that exact moment of the first cry? It we are off by\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain l minutes, we have made an error of less than one degree in the ascendant of the chart we\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain are about to erect. It is quite common for astrologers to try to gain an accuracy of minutes\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain and seconds and never think of questioning the accuracy of the recorded birth time itself.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Now, let us assume that the above birth was 11:29 A.M. instead of ll:29 P.M. That would mean\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain that it was 31 minutes before Noon. So; instead of adding the birth time to the Sidereal Time\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of 4:45 at Noon, we would deduct 31 minutes from 4:45 and get a Sidereal Time at birth of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 4:14. Some students get confused as to whether they should add or subtract. If this happens\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain to you, you can do it another way, and you will always be adding.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Instead of deducting 31 minutes, let us add the birth time, even though it is an A.M. birth. If\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the Sidereal Time was 4:45 at Noon, add 12 hours and it will be 16:45 at midnight, and the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain birth was 11 hours and 29 minutes after midnight it occurred at 11:29 A.M.\par
\pard\f0\fs22\b SIDEREAL TIME AT NOON ....4:45\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f0\fs22\b NOON TO MIDNIGHT ............12:00\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f0\fs22\b TIME OF BIRTH .....................11:29\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain
\pard\f1\fs20\plain \u9642?\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 27:74\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Here again, we can change 27:74 to 28:l4, because there are only 60 minutes in an hour, but\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain now we have a figure that is greater than 24 hours, and since Sidereal Time runs only up to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain 24:00 hours, we deduct 24:00 from 28:l4, getting 4:14, exactly the same answer we obtained\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain above. You can choose either method, the one that suits you best.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Be sure you understand this Lesson. Any error or misconception here might be difficult to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain overcome later.\par
