\pard\f1\fs20\plain Analytical InterpretationIn opening a discussion of the interpretation of planetary aspects, it is necessary to introduce\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the planetary nodes. The majority of the astrologers of the early part of the twentieth\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain century have ignored these nodes, although they were very important to the ancients, as is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain indicated by the terms "Dragon's Head and Dragon's Tail applied to the north or ascending\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain node and the south or descending node of the Moon. Today we call them the lunar nodes.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It is quite easy to understand why they are ignored when we realize that these astrologers\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain were trying to bridge the gap to what was fashionable in the world of so-called science. They\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain were trying to adjust to the environment They were trying to fit their subject into the errors\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of scientific style. Like so many other things, scientific theories and hypotheses come and go,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain live for a day and die. At that time, it was popular to think in terms of the ether of Sir Oliver\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Lodge, in terms of vibrations and waves. So astrologers had to explain their subject in terms\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of ether, vibrations and waves. It was pointed out that when light reached the place or point\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of a planetary node, there was nothing there for light to bounce against, and so light went\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain right on. How could a mathematical point have an effect? And so the nodes were forgotten\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain and ignored.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Grant Lewi placed great emphasis on the nodes. Statistics of RCA Communications, Inc., in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain connection with their research dealing with the correspondence that exists between\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain planetary motion and magnetic disturbances that interfere with shortwave radio reception,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain placed emphasis on the nodes. Letters from two astrologers unknown to each other asked us\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the same question. Did we know why people born with the Sun near 13-Gemini or 13-\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Sagittarius seemed to have so much difficulty in making marriage a success? Of course, this\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain meant success according to orthodox standards which are quite inadequate, but it was\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain interesting to note that each astrologer had unconsciously called the exact position of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain nodes of the planet Uranus (Individual Reform Guide and Seventh House principle). Marriage\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain is difficult where this principle is concerned because marriage is a Man-made inst;itution that\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain was molded in ignorance of this principle. When he wrote the Ten Commandments General\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Moses had his intellectual limitations.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Our own statistical investigations gradually led to indications that mere mathematical points\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain (we will supplement this term later), the planetary nodes, are more important than the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain planets themselves, and this was the beginning of our realization that an entirely new\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain conception of astrology was essential, an astrology unlimited and unhampered by the dogma\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of modern scientific hypotheses. When you deal with mathematical points, mathematical lines\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain and mathematical planes which can have no material counterpart, you are in the realms of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain mathematics and abstraction. A mathematical point has no dimensions. A mathematical line\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain has but one dimension. A mathematical plane has but two dimensions. There is no material\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain counterpart until you reach three dimensions, and when you go on to a fourth dimension, you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain again have no material counterpart. You have again entered the world of abstraction.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain You can use a pencil to illustrate a mathematical line, but it is inaccurate, because a pencil line\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain has width and therefore two dimensions. You can use a sheet of paper to illustrate a plane,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain but it is inaccurate because the paper has thickness and is three dimensional.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We were approachIng our later view that astrology could not be a causal phenomenon; but\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain must be considered as a matter of mathematical expression. We cannot exp1ain why\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain snowflakes and crystals follow the same pattern that bees follow when building the cells of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain their hives (the hexagon) except to say that the whole thing is a matter of abstract\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain mathematical expression. Although the hexagon is the most economical means of building\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain these cells, we can hardly visualize each hive of bees employing intellectual bees who labor\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain over a drawing board computing the mathematics of what shaped room would require the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain least wall space to house the greatest content. Neither can we say that snowflakes cause\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain bees to build six-sided cells. Nor can we say that snowflakes freeze in six-sided fashion\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain because they are copying the bees. Yet a hexagon room enclosed by 60 feet of wall will\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain contain over 259 square feet of floor area, while a square room enclosed by the same\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain amount of wall will contain but 225 square feet of floor space. A room shaped as an\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain equilateral triangle with 60 feet of wall will contain only 141 square feet of area. Of course, a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain circular room would contain a greater ratio of area to enclosure, but you can't fit circular\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain rooms together. The more equal sides a room has, the greater the floor area, but if a room\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain has more than six sides, you can't fit the rooms together. Among polygons of equal sides you\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain can fit together only hexagons, squares and triangles.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Mathematically, the bee would appear further advanced than Man. He builds warehouses out\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of hexagonal shaped rooms while Man builds warehouses out of square or rectangular rooms.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We find a strange parallel in the fact that the ancient astrologers regarded planetary aspects\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain based on parts of hexagons as harmonious and aspects based on parts of squares as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain discordant. The sextile aspect is merely one side of a hexagon, while a trine aspect is one\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain side of an equilateral triangle or two sides of a hexagon. An opposition aspect can be\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain considered as either two squares or three sides of a hexagon, and oddly, astrologers have\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain disagreed as to whether the opposition aspect is harmonious or discordant. It has been our\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain experience that the aspect brings tension, problems and worries while forming, but produces\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain CHANGE that solves the problems and gives re-lief while separating. If there is any\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain astrological symbolism behind the six-pointed star of the Jewish church and the four-point\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain cross of the Christian church, then one would appear to Indicate harmony and the other\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain discord. Yet, the advanced astrologer realizes that the l, 10, 7, 4 House Cross (Leo, Scorpio,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Aquarius, Taurus) is something that can be transcended through understanding and higher\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain knowledge or wisdom, while those astrologers who look upon the story of Christ as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain symbolism see Christ, not as a man but as an abstract principle. To them, it is the rise of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain individual above materialism and into the abstract realms. Whether Christ ever lived as a man\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain is beside the point. The principle is the same.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Just as astrology is concerned with geometrical and therefore mathmatical figures, we find\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain these figures throughout the mineral world. Different minerals are made up of crystals of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain different geometrical patterns. Not all minerals follow the hexagonal pattern. Salt crystals\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain form the square or 90-degree angle. This is true of other minerals, but the hexagonal pattern\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain is quite common. The crystal mines of Arkansas seem to show everything in accord with the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain hexagonal prinqiple. Where erosion had washed away a patch of a lava bed at the floor of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the Grand Canyon, we found petrifted wood covered by hexagonal crystals. At the Spring\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Creek Ranch in the Texas Panhandle are found strange objects composed of calcite an inch\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain thick and two inches in diameter that are in the form of a perfect hexagon. They are said to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain be a hang over from the Triassic Age, which was the earliest period of the Mesozoic Era. By\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain careful selection,you could pave a bathroom floor with these strange objects, but most of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain them have other hexagons growing out of them at various angles. A geode is a strange\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain object of nature that looks like an egg and is shaped like an egg. Their is a location near\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Mammouth Arizona, where these eggs lying on top of the ground in quantity over an area of\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain ten or more square miles. These are agate geodes. They are white on the outside like an egg.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Originally, they appear to have been mere shells, but from the inner surface grow crystals\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain until the object finally becomes a solid. A hollow ultimately becomes a solid. Where does the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain material come from? Or does material need to come from some place? When it is ready to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain appear it seems to appear. Astronomers are trying to learn how matter appears or forms in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain space. How does it appear or form in the hollow of these geodes?\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain An astrological aspect is merely an abstract geometrical measurement. True, it is sometimes a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain measurement between two material objects, but this need not be true. It can be a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain measurement between a planet and a mathematical point, or a measurement between two\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain mathematical points where there is nothing whatsoever of a material nature. It can still have\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain its counterpart in human affairs.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Because the planetary nodes are even more important than the planets tnemselves, it is\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain important that you understand what a planetary node is. Remember that we used imaginary\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain embroidery hoops to illustrate two planes of space. Put one hoop inside the other, twist one\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain so that one hoop crosses the other at two points. Look at the hoops from above that point\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain where they cross and you see an X. The point where the two hoops cross is the node. The\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain other point where they cross is also a node. It is customary to call one point the north node,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the other point the south node.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain What we call the north node of a planet is that point where the orbit of a planet crosses the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain plane of the earth's orbit as the planet goes north. The south node is the other point, and\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain when the planet crosses the south node, it is traveling southerly.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Actually, no planet ever crosses the earth's orbit. It crosses the plane of the earth's orbit,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain Therefore it is not strictly true to consider these factors as mathematical points. When\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain referring to a node, an astronomer means that mathematical point where the plane of a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain planet cuts the earth's orbit, but we could as well consIder the node that mathematical point\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain where the plane of the earth's orbit cuts the planetary orbit. These two points would not be\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the same in space. One would be at the orbit of the earth while the other would be at the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain orbit of the planet. However, if we draw a straight line from the Sun to the mathematical\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain point that is furthest away from the Sun, it would of necessity have to pass through the other\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain mathematical point. Thus, the important factor with which we deal becomes not a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain mathematical point, but a mathematical line drawn from the Sun in opposite directions to\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain infinity. The plane of the earth's orbit and the plane of the planetary orbit join all the way\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain along the line.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain As computed today, the planetary nodes are considered as heliocentric, those points where\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the above described line crosses the earth's orbit. However, if we use these points as if they\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain were geocentric, they appear to work. This is easy to understand if we consicer that we are\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain dealing with mathematical lines instead of mathematical points. Now, we can draw a new set\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain of lines with the earth as the center to those points where the line above described reaches\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain infinity and their direction must be exactly the same as the direction from the Sun. Such a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain conception justifies us in using the planetary nodes in our zodiac just as they are given in\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain heliocentric longitude.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain This view might also completely justify Hugh MacCraig and others who are employing\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain heliocentric positions in the earth zodiac.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We know from experience that when the nodes are employed in this fashion they function.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain We do not know whether they will also function when located by Iines from the earth as a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain center to those points where the nodal line crosses the earth's orbit, for these points have\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain never been calculated and published. A student well schooled in mathematics is now\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain calculating these positions, however, and we hope it will ultimately be possible to publish\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain them for experimental purposes.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain It becomes vital to know the zodiacal position of the planetary nodes. The position of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain north lunar node is given in the ephemeris. The south lunar node will be at the opposite\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain zodiacal point. The Sun is not involved in this calculation, because the Moon goes around the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain earth. The point where the two orbits cross is itself in motion, and it circumvents the entire\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain circle of the zodiac in 19 years.\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain The planetary nodes have a similar motion, but it is too slow to be significant within a\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain lifetime. The nodes of Mercury circle the zodiac once in something over 171,000 years, while\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain the nodes of Uranus make the trip in about 40,000 years. Oddly, the nodes of some of the\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain outer planets appear to move more rapidly than those of the inner planets. In any event,\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain however, no planetary node moves a degree within 100 years, so you can use the present\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain nodal positions as close enough for any living being. Present north nodal positions are as\par
\pard\f1\fs20\plain follows:\par
